25.text:
 An external publication can retain original source in Git while storing its extracted facts in UIR. PublishedDocument.PathKey remains the producer's logical document path. PublishedDocument.GitPath names the original file relative to the repository root. These paths can differ. Set ExternalLocation.RepositoryURI and a resolved PublishOptions.GitCommit when any document has GitPath. Keep supplying the exact original bytes in Source and their SHA-256 in ContentHash during publication; UIR validates that pair before writing. A generated or transformed document leaves GitPath empty and uses the existing source blob store. One publication can contain both kinds. publication.Location.RepositoryURI = "/path/to/rules.git"
publication.Options = indexer.PublishOptions{
    Historical: true,
    GitCommit:  commitSHA,
    Producer:   producerFingerprint,
}
publication.Documents[0].GitPath = "rules/pc/Example/XMLData.xml"
result, err := indexer.Publish(ctx, database, publication) The binding is part of source revision identity and publication hashes. Moving the original file creates a new binding even when its bytes and logical path are unchanged. Existing snapshots retain their original binding. A registered external location cannot change repositories; give a different repository a distinct location URI. Pipeline.ReadModuleSources resolves the snapshot's bindings, retrieves original files at the pinned commit, and verifies every returned SHA-256. Local absolute paths and file:// URIs accept working repositories and bare clones. Reads use NUL-delimited native Git batches, including paths containing tabs or newlines; Git 2.42 or newer is required. A missing object, failed reader, or mismatched hash is an error. Applications with authenticated remote repositories provide their existing cache through query.WithGitSourceReader. The callback receives the repository URI, immutable commit, and repository-relative paths. It must return bytes in the same order. UIR checks result count and content hashes independently. pipeline, err := query.NewPipeline(database,
    query.WithGitSourceReader(readFromRepositoryCache),
)
// readFromRepositoryCache has type query.GitSourceReader:
// func(context.Context, query.GitSourceOptions) ([][]byte, error) Historical publication continues to preserve location heads. Generated manifests can pin the returned module snapshot IDs using the existing source/blob and snapshot model; Git source bindings require no separate catalog database.

26.text:
 An external historical publication can supply a delta against Options.BaseSnapshotID. Set Delta and provide every document of each changed package. Packages omitted entirely retain their existing document membership, source revisions and source metrics without loading their bytes. List deleted paths explicitly, including every removed file of a package that no longer exists. publication.Options.BaseSnapshotID = previous.SnapshotID
publication.Delta = &indexer.PublicationDelta{
    DeletedPaths: []string{"retired/XMLData.xml", "retired/metadata.json"},
}
publication.Documents = changedPackageDocuments
result, err := indexer.Publish(ctx, database, publication) The base must have the same root, producer configuration, repository origin and indexed coverage. Partial packages, missing bases, unknown deletions and replacing and deleting the same path are errors before publication. An empty delta can retain all packages at a new commit. Without Delta, Documents remains a complete replacement. Historical heads stay unchanged in both cases. Generated source reads use bounded SQL batches through storage.ReadSourceBlobs. Every stored hash is verified, and a missing or corrupt blob fails the read. Original Git sources continue through the configured Git reader in a native batch.

