359.text:
 The DSN selects PostgreSQL (URL or keyword DSN) or SQLite (sqlite:// URL or plain .db path). Schema selects a PostgreSQL schema and defaults to public; SQLite rejects a non-default schema. In-memory SQLite is rejected because migration and application connections would not reliably share state. SQLite enables foreign keys, WAL, a five-second busy timeout, and one open connection. PostgreSQL is the shared-writer option. Migration and a connection ping must succeed before UirDB returns. database, err := storage.UirDB(ctx, storage.DBOptions{DSN: "./state/uir.db"})
if err != nil {
    return err
}
sqlDB, err := database.DB()
if err != nil {
    return err
}
defer sqlDB.Close() Opening an existing database discards the index of every previous schema generation. UirDB opens the connection, discards a pre-handle, H64a, or two-layer fingerprint index, applies the current schema, inserts the builtin symbol kinds it lacks, records the handle layout, and then discards the uir_-prefixed generations. The pre-handle, H64a, and two-layer fingerprint cutovers are one mechanism (storage/handle_cutover.go). One catalog query classifies the database: no modules table is empty; modules without ordinal is pre-handle; modules with ordinal but no symbol_layout row is H64a; a symbol_layout row equal to symbolhandle.Layout (H64b) with a symbol_deltas.shape_fp column is two-layer fingerprint, and without it current; any other recorded layout is an error naming it, because that database was written by another uir version. Every stale generation drops the same list of index tables, child first, skipping any the generation never had: symbol_deltas, symbol_postings, snapshot_dependencies, package_coverage, documents, symbols, symbol_packages, symbol_modules, source_deltas, source_revisions, location_heads, snapshots, and source_blobs. modules, locations, primary_locations, task_runs, and symbol_kinds keep their rows. A kept task run may list snapshot ids the cutover discarded. H64a generation. A database built before H64b handles packed kind into 3 bits and local into 32, checked symbols.kind against the eight Go kinds, and has no symbol_kinds or symbol_layout. Its handles mean nothing in H64b, so the cutover drops the index tables above in one transaction before migration, and migration creates them again with symbols.kind referencing symbol_kinds. Registered roots keep their ordinals. The refusals, the SQLite foreign-key suspension, and the warning are those of the pre-handle cutover below, with "H64a" in place of "pre-handle"; OpenReadOnly fails with "database predates symbol handle layout H64b; open it read-write once or run uir reindex". The test fixture testdata/h64a/ holds that generation's 04–09 HCL. Two-layer fingerprint generation. A database built before layered symbol fingerprints has symbol_deltas with shape_fp and body_fp and format 4 documents with shape_hash and body_hash; neither converts into the public, private, and content layers without re-extracting. The cutover drops the index tables above before migration, which then creates symbol_deltas as 13_layered_fingerprints.hcl declares it. Registered roots keep their ordinals; the refusals, the SQLite foreign-key suspension, and the warning are those of the pre-handle cutover, with "two-layer fingerprint" in place of "pre-handle"; OpenReadOnly fails with "database predates layered symbol fingerprints; open it read-write once or run uir reindex". fingerprint_cutover_integration_test.go builds the generation by replacing a current database's symbol_deltas with the two-fingerprint table. Pre-handle generation. A database built before compact symbol handles has the same unprefixed table names, but modules, snapshots, and documents have no ordinal, symbols has no handle, and symbol_postings is keyed by (document_id, symbol_id, role). The current schema cannot be reconciled onto those tables without rewriting their rows, so this cutover runs before migration. Such a database is recognised by modules existing without an ordinal column: one catalog query (sqlite_master and pragma_table_info on SQLite, pg_class and pg_attribute in the Schema on PostgreSQL), which is the only cost it adds when opening a current or empty database. In one transaction UirDB then: Discards the index. It drops symbol_postings, package_coverage, documents, symbols, source_deltas, source_revisions, location_heads, and snapshots, child first, and migration creates them again, empty. No index rows are imported. location_heads goes with them because every head references a snapshot.Keeps the registrations. locations and primary_locations are unchanged in the current schema and keep their rows. modules keeps its rows and gains ordinal, numbered densely from 1 by created_at, then root_key. On PostgreSQL that is ADD COLUMN, an UPDATE from ROW_NUMBER(), and SET NOT NULL; migration then adds the unique key and the check. SQLite can add neither a NOT NULL column nor a check to a populated table, so the cutover rebuilds modules as 04_module_roots.hcl declares it (the CREATE TABLE commons-db would emit), copies the rows with their ordinals, and renames it into place; migration then adds the two unique indexes. Run uir reindex --all afterwards; UirDB logs a warning saying so. Every registered location is headless, so reindex indexes it in full: a location's first snapshot bases on the primary location's head, and on none when the primary has no head either. Until a root is reindexed, uir list and uir get show it with no snapshot, and unscoped readers skip it with a warning, while an explicit root or checkout selection still fails with "root … is registered but not indexed; run uir reindex on its checkout". On SQLite the cutover runs with foreign key enforcement suspended on its one connection and restored afterwards: enforcement makes DROP TABLE symbols check every row against the unindexed self-reference owner_id (about three minutes on the fixture corpus, a few seconds without), and would refuse to drop the old modules, which locations references. The same refusals apply as below, to the discarded tables: SQLite checks every other table for a foreign key to one of them, PostgreSQL refuses to drop a table another object depends on, and either failure rolls the whole cutover back and leaves the pre-handle tables and their rows unmigrated. Reopening the rebuilt database finds modules.ordinal and changes nothing. OpenReadOnly runs the same detection and fails with "database predates compact symbol handles; open it read-write once or run uir reindex". The test fixture testdata/prehandle/ holds that generation's 04–06 HCL. uir_-prefixed generations. After applying the current schema, UirDB drops only the uir_-prefixed module-root generation (uir_source_deltas, uir_module_location_heads, uir_module_primaries, uir_source_revisions, uir_module_snapshots, uir_module_locations, uir_module_roots) and the older project generation (uir_relationships, uir_fields, uir_node_locations, uir_nodes, uir_sources, uir_roots, uir_project_heads, uir_snapshots, uir_projects), child first, in one transaction. No rows are imported: run uir reindex on each checkout to rebuild the index. Back up an old database before opening it with this version if its history matters. PostgreSQL refuses a drop blocked by an external dependency; SQLite checks nonlegacy tables for foreign keys to legacy tables and refuses the cutover. Initialization then fails, and every legacy table remains, instead of silently removing dependent data. Migration and cutover are separate transactions: if cutover fails, the new schema may already exist while legacy tables remain. Reopening a successfully cut-over database has no legacy tables left to drop. The HCL files are one schema source for both targets. PostgreSQL uses canonical uuid, jsonb, and timestamptz; the SQLite adapter in commons-db/migrate maps them to text, JSON-valid text, and datetime while preserving keys, checks, and indexes. The commons-db/migrate/README.md guide defines the portable HCL subset and incompatibilities. UIR permits transactional SQLite rebuilds for compatible constraint changes, including widening symbol_postings_role_check from roles 0–2 to 0–3 while preserving postings, and widening locations_kind_check and snapshots_reason_check to admit external and import while preserving every location and snapshot (PostgreSQL replaces the two constraints in place). Drops, renames, and type changes remain refused. The compact-handle columns (modules.ordinal, snapshots.ordinal, symbols.handle, documents.ordinal) and the reshaped symbol_postings were added to 04 and 06 in place; a database that holds rows under the previous shape has its index discarded and its registrations numbered by the pre-handle cutover above rather than migrated.

